WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.587 --> 00:00:06.464
What do saber-toothed tigers,

00:00:06.464 --> 00:00:07.590
woolly mammoths

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:09.634
and the dodo all have in common?

00:00:09.634 --> 00:00:12.512
They're all extinct.

00:00:12.512 --> 00:00:16.891
And some theorize humans
played a large part in this.

00:00:29.821 --> 00:00:33.158
This is WHAT IF, and here's what would happen

00:00:33.158 --> 00:00:36.327
if extinct animals could be brought back to life.

00:00:36.327 --> 00:00:39.622
One day, this might not be a hypothetical,

00:00:39.622 --> 00:00:43.752
as science is getting closer
to bringing extinct animals back.

00:00:43.752 --> 00:00:49.507
Known as de-extinction, it's the process of
bringing extinct animals back from the dead.

00:00:49.507 --> 00:00:54.012
What used to be science fiction
is slowly becoming a reality.

00:00:54.012 --> 00:00:56.848
It's possible that this might happen one day but,

00:00:56.848 --> 00:01:00.602
unfortunately, you won't be seeing any dinosaurs.

00:01:00.602 --> 00:01:01.770
Why?

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:05.982
Well, that's because, in order for
this de-extinction process to work,

00:01:05.982 --> 00:01:09.527
 a species' DNA needs to be of a certain age.

00:01:09.527 --> 00:01:14.407
 Scientists say that DNA becomes
unreadable after 1.5 million years, 

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:17.077
after that amount of time it's practically useless.

00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:22.832
So dinosaurs that went extinct 65 million
years ago are ineligible for this experiment.

00:01:22.832 --> 00:01:24.626
Sorry.

00:01:24.626 --> 00:01:28.546
But if the species is young enough,
and the DNA happens to be readable,

00:01:28.546 --> 00:01:31.883
then scientists can use it for genome editing.

00:01:31.883 --> 00:01:35.804
One of the ways this could work would be
using the DNA from extinct species

00:01:35.804 --> 00:01:39.724
 and fusing it with its closest animal
relative currently living today.

00:01:39.724 --> 00:01:44.229
One of the animals that scientists
say is eligible for de-extinction? 

00:01:44.229 --> 00:01:45.772
The woolly mammoth.

00:01:45.772 --> 00:01:49.651
Small mammoth populations were
still alive just 3,600 years ago.

00:01:49.651 --> 00:01:53.863
Experts believe that this species
could have survived extinction

00:01:53.863 --> 00:01:56.032
 if it wasn't for humans hunting them.

00:01:56.032 --> 00:01:59.327
One potential way mammoth
de-extinction could work

00:01:59.327 --> 00:02:04.958
 is if scientists were to combine woolly
mammoth DNA with that of elephants alive today. 

00:02:04.958 --> 00:02:06.835
Over time these hybrid animals

00:02:06.835 --> 00:02:11.840
could slowly morph into full-on mammoths,
effectively bringing the species back.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:14.092
This might take a few thousand years but,

00:02:14.092 --> 00:02:16.886
over time mammoths could be
roaming the Earth again.

00:02:16.886 --> 00:02:19.013
If this method were to work, 

00:02:19.013 --> 00:02:22.725
think of all the incredible animals
we could have back in our world.

00:02:22.725 --> 00:02:27.897
The 12-foot-tall giant
short-faced bear, hairless dogs,

00:02:27.897 --> 00:02:30.984
and of course the loveable dodo bird.

00:02:30.984 --> 00:02:34.946
Just remember to be wary of the saber-toothed
tigers that would be running around.

00:02:34.946 --> 00:02:37.240
You don't want to mess with those.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:40.952
 If we wanted to get weird,
we could even bring back Neanderthals,

00:02:40.952 --> 00:02:46.124
although that could be incredibly
dangerous and would ultimately be a stupid idea. 

00:02:46.124 --> 00:02:48.543
This species would die pretty quickly as they

00:02:48.543 --> 00:02:51.171
just wouldn't be able to survive in our current world.

00:02:51.171 --> 00:02:55.758
With our diets, germs, and diseases,
Neanderthals wouldn't last long.

00:02:55.758 --> 00:02:58.720
That goes for all these other
animals we're bringing back too.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:02.390
Sure, in theory, they
should be able to adapt over time,

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:06.853
 but there are tons of other issues that may
come with bringing a species back from extinction.

00:03:06.853 --> 00:03:11.024
One thing we'd need to be worried about
is just how do we control these species?

00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:14.694
 We wouldn't want a giant short-faced
 bear mating with other bears -

00:03:14.694 --> 00:03:17.197
that could present a number of unknown issues.

00:03:17.197 --> 00:03:20.241
But what's to stop them from doing it anyway?

00:03:20.241 --> 00:03:25.079
Another question science would need
to ask is what exactly is the point of all this?

00:03:25.079 --> 00:03:30.084
 Sure being able to ride a woolly mammoth
would be amazing but is it really worth it?

00:03:30.084 --> 00:03:35.173
What kind of purpose are these animals
serving other than the sake of our entertainment?

00:03:35.173 --> 00:03:41.512
And there exists the possibility that these new animals
would disrupt the current ecosystems we have today, 

00:03:41.512 --> 00:03:45.099
leading to other animals
going extinct because of them.

00:03:45.099 --> 00:03:47.894
One good thing de-extinction could do is

00:03:47.894 --> 00:03:51.898
bring back essential animals we
have today that might become extinct.

00:03:51.898 --> 00:03:56.402
Bees have long been talked about as
a species that may go extinct relatively soon.

00:03:56.402 --> 00:04:01.574
If de-extinction technology is finally realized,
maybe we'll never need to worry about it happening.

00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:04.077
But that also raises some concern.

00:04:04.077 --> 00:04:08.289
If that was our reality, people may
think it was okay for things to go extinct

00:04:08.289 --> 00:04:10.458
since we could always bring them back.

00:04:10.458 --> 00:04:16.339
This could cause an increase in hunting, and a
general carelessness for animals all around the world.

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:20.176
Bringing back a species
may sound like an incredible idea but,

00:04:20.176 --> 00:04:23.471
the risks and unpredictability of it are huge.

00:04:23.471 --> 00:04:26.766
Maybe it's just best to
take care of what we have now

00:04:26.766 --> 00:04:29.644
and make sure it doesn't happen again.

00:04:29.644 --> 00:04:33.398
But what if everyone and
everything went extinct, but you?

00:04:33.398 --> 00:04:39.320
Well, that sounds like a question for another WHAT IF.

